// Generated by scripts/restructure-by-construct.
// Per-construct DSL layout: every <kind>s.memql file in a domain
// holds every construct of that kind in the domain. The companion
// files cover the other construct kinds.

// A POLICY SAYS WHERE TO LOOK; A RULE SAYS WHICH CALLS IT IS FOR.
// The rules are in dsl/rules/rules.memql. Nothing in this file decides which
// calls it serves; rules select these reusable chains
// without duplicating a chain at every call site.
//
// THE ORDER INSIDE A CHAIN IS THREE KINDS OF COST, INCREASING:
//
//   fleet:      a model on hardware the person already owns. Costs electricity.
//   app:        a signed-in Claude Code or Codex on one of their machines.
//               Costs a subscription they already pay for; MemQL is not billed.
//   federation: a metered vendor. Costs money, and is therefore the hop the
//               cost ceiling gates -- and gates only when a local door preceded
//               it, because a chain that STARTS at a vendor is a decision
//               somebody made rather than a fallback.
//
// `fleet:strongest` AND `app:*` ARE SELECTORS, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THIS
// SHIPPABLE AS A DEFAULT. A policy naming one model id would park on every
// fleet running something else, and which weights a person pulled is not
// knowable here. A selector resolves per call, among the models that can serve
// THAT call. `fleet:*` was the old spelling of `fleet:strongest` and is
// retired: it said "any", which is not what it did.
//
// A POLICY NOTHING CAN NAME IS A DECORATION. That rule cost this file four
// policies once already and six more in the epic that wrote it. It has no
// exemptions now: `localOnly` was the one, held open as the VOCABULARY a
// custom rule could point at, and epic memql#5137's `compilerLocalOnly` names
// it -- so the rule that compiles a person's sentence about where their work
// may run is itself pinned to their own hardware. It is still the policy an
// owner writing "this does not leave my machines" points at; it is no longer
// only that.

/// The default chain: local strongest, then a signed-in app on the person's own
/// machine, then the cheapest federated model that qualifies. Used by ordinary
/// calls outside the fast, reasoning and embedding lanes.
///
/// `federation:cheapest` orders by input plus output cost per million ascending
/// among the records that can serve the call. A vendor record with no cost
/// figures sorts LAST and the decision record says so -- sorting it first would
/// make a price nobody filled in the cheapest thing in the catalog.
@primary("fleet:strongest")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("federation:cheapest")
policy localFirst { }

/// Fast calls prefer the smallest eligible local model above the selector's
/// quality floor, then an app, then the cheapest qualifying federated model.
/// The floor applies to the whole local chain, including fallback candidates.
@primary("fleet:fastest")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("federation:cheapest")
policy fastLocalFirst { }

/// Local only, with no fallback authored on purpose. When the fleet cannot
/// serve the call the rule's onUnavailable decides, and every door being shut
/// is the honest answer rather than a vendor call the person did not ask for.
///
/// Named by `compilerLocalOnly`, and it is the policy a CUSTOM rule points at
/// when an owner wants work pinned to their own hardware.
@primary("fleet:strongest")
policy localOnly { }

/// Local strongest, then an app, then the STRONGEST federated model rather than
/// the cheapest. Named by reasoningParks, for work where a wrong answer costs
/// more than a dear one.
///
/// It is still paid-last: the vendor hop is third and the cost ceiling gates
/// it exactly as it gates localFirst's. What differs is which vendor record
/// wins once the chain gets there.
@primary("fleet:strongest")
@fallback("app:*")
@fallback("federation:strongest")
policy federationStrongest { }

/// The cluster's bound embedder, and nothing after it (epic memql#5137).
///
/// A ONE-ENTRY CHAIN IS THE POINT, and it is the one place in this file where
/// a fallback would be a defect rather than a kindness. Every other policy here
/// degrades to a weaker model that gives a worse answer to the same question.
/// An embedder does not do that: a different embedder answers in a DIFFERENT
/// VECTOR SPACE, so its output does not belong in the index it is about to be
/// written to. A fallback would fill `node_vectors_<dims>` with vectors that
/// are not comparable to the ones already in it, and nothing downstream can
/// tell -- every subsequent similarity read returns plausible neighbours that
/// are simply wrong.
///
/// `embedder:active` resolves to whatever the operator BOUND, which may be a
/// fleet model or a federated one; the door is derived from the ref rather
/// than stated here, so binding a local embedder and binding OpenAI's are the
/// same authoring act.
@primary("embedder:active")
policy embeddingsBinding { }

/// Research can use the tools inside existing signed-in subscription apps.
/// Each concrete app is a separate fallback, so a quota refusal from one does
/// not hide the other behind a wildcard. No metered provider is in this chain.
/// Balanced models bound research cost. DSL selects app-first or independent
/// parallel work and uses local research when these app sources are unavailable.
@primary("app:codex:gpt-5.6-terra")
@fallback("app:claude-code:sonnet")
policy researchApps { }
